--TEST--
`getSecret`'s `fill` supplies a miss once and every later caller reads the entry it stored, which is the one `putSecret` writes — `rule:concurrency/a-secret-fill-runs-once-per-process`, `rule:concurrency/put-and-get-are-the-whole-boundary`, `rule:core-api/shape-rules`
--FILE--
<?nvs
secret bytes $key = Core\Crypto::generateKey();
array<secret bytes> $ring = [$key];

var $store = Core\Cache::process();

// A miss with no `fill` written is a miss, unchanged: the bag's options are
// what a caller adds to `getSecret`, never what it needs to write to read one.
echo "a miss with no fill: ", $store->getSecret("api-token", $ring) == null ? "null" : "something", "\n";

// The fill runs in this caller's own request, and what it answers carries both
// halves a fetch learned — `rule:core-api/shape-rules` R14's object, because a
// token endpoint is the authority on how long its own token stays good.
var $fetch = fn (): Core\Cache\SecretEntry => {
    echo "fetching\n";
    return Core\Cache\SecretEntry::of("t0ken", 1h);
};
var $first = $store->getSecret("api-token", $ring, {fill: $fetch});
echo "the fill supplied the miss: ", ($first ?? "") == "t0ken" ? "yes" : "no", "\n";

// And every later caller reads what that one stored rather than fetching
// again. This fill answers a different secret and prints on its way, so a
// member that ran it would say both things here.
var $again = fn (): Core\Cache\SecretEntry => {
    echo "fetching again\n";
    return Core\Cache\SecretEntry::of("another", 1h);
};
var $second = $store->getSecret("api-token", $ring, {fill: $again});
echo "a later caller reads what it stored: ", ($second ?? "") == "t0ken" ? "yes" : "no", "\n";

// What a fill leaves behind is an ordinary sealed entry: the same seal
// `putSecret` writes, so the plain `get` door finds nothing under the name.
echo "and the entry is sealed like any other: ", $store->get("api-token") == null ? "null" : "something", "\n";
--EXPECT--
a miss with no fill: null
fetching
the fill supplied the miss: yes
a later caller reads what it stored: yes
and the entry is sealed like any other: null
